TY  - JOUR
T1  - Depression and suicide: What an evidence‐based clinician should know
AN  - 2869681346; 2023-14278-001
AB  - This article discusses the special issue of 
Clinical Psychology & Psychotherapy which focuses on depression and suicide. In the first article in this Special Issue, a meta-ethnography of 21 qualitative studies exploring Mindfulness-based Cognitive Therapy for people with major depression is reported. Second article provides a systematic review and meta-analysis of empirical studies evaluating whether or not remitted depression is associated with retrieving fewer specific and more over general autobiographical memories. The third article presents a systematic literature review on hospital presenting suicidal ideation. The findings from this review show that people presenting to Emergency Departments with suicidal ideation vary in age, gender, ethnic background and socio-economic status. Importantly, previous suicide attempt appears to be a risk factor in such presentations. The authors' review identifies key gaps and weaknesses in the literature and a clear need for future research. In the fourth article, a randomized controlled clinical trial is published in which Meta-Cognitive Training for Depression in acute-psychiatric inpatient care is investigated in Germany. The fifth article in this issue, rates of sudden gain occurrence, pretreatment factors and posttreatment outcomes were examined in US patients who received daily, intensive therapy. In the sixth article of this issue the role of mood and affect as a proximal risk factor of suicidal ideation was investigated. This was done by using Ecological Momentary Assessment (EMA) in a high-risk sample of depressed patients in Germany with current and/or lifestime suicidal ideation. In the seventh article of this issue, the relationships between attachment insecurity and suicidal ideation and behaviour were examined in the UK in a sample of individuals with experience of suicidal ideation. The last article of this issue, examines the role of SOC in recovery and loss in elderly spousally bereaved people in Denmark using a longitudinal research design. The practical implication of this work is that helping bereaved people to experience life's demands is likely to be an important target for bereavement care. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
