TY  - JOUR
T1  - Fronto-executive functions
AN  - 2450308074; 2020-75726-001
AB  - The idea for this special issue was to try to recruit researchers who work on fronto-executive functions, but through different areas (i.e., attention, social, emotional, etc.) in both humans and laboratory animals to try to create a synthesis of how next generation neuroscientists can think about future work in this area. The present issue reflects new directions in the study of the development of fronto-executive functions in normal children and those with neurodevelopmental disorders. Zelazo and Carlson (2020) provide an extensive review of the development of executive functions; Okerstrom-Jezewski et al. (2020) describe how early prefrontal injury to the dorsomedial prefrontal region alters behavior and resting state functional magnetic resonance imaging; de Jong and Smit (2020) explore the effectiveness of working memory training in adolescents at risk for school dropout; Zamora, Richards, Juric, Aydmune, and Introzzi (2020) investigate inhibitory processes in children; and, Neuwirth, Lopez, Schneider, and Markowitz (2020) review the effects of neurodevelopmental lead exposure on fronto-executive functions. Three papers investigated fronto-executive functions in aging. Argiris, MacPherson, Della Sala, and Foley (2020) studied the effect of aging on different tests of executive function; Blujus et al. (2020) examined whether contextual olfactory cues could improve frontal-dependent memory aging humans and rats; and Iveson, Della Sala, and MacPherson (2020) investigated prefrontal functions in aging. Two papers investigated the potential use of new measures of fronto-executive functions for nonclinical and clinical applications. The Afonso, Machado, Carreiro, and Machado-Pinheiro (2020) paper was directed at English-speaking participants, whereas the Miranda, Rivadero, Serra, and Soria (2020) paper examined the development of a new executive questionnaire that is culturally relevant in Hispanic-speaking populations. The remaining papers all examined fronto-executive functions in rats with a review of the role of prefrontal cortex in regulating fear learning and extinction (Gonzalez & Fanselow, 2020), a study of the relative roles of glutamatergic and serotonergic modulation of prefrontal cortex in reversal learning (Hervig, Piilgaard, Božicˇ, Alsiö, & Robbins, 2020) and a look at the effect of the chronic effects of methylphenidate treatment during development (Comeau & Kolb, 2020). We hope that this diverse set of papers will provide readers with a rich well of ideas and techniques that will stimulate thinking and research into the study of fronto-executive functions in the future. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
