TY  - JOUR
T1  - Cognitive training improves cognitive performance, but what else?
AN  - 2214503691; 2018-17657-002
AB  - Comments on an article by S. Belleville et al. (see record 2018-17657-005). Belleville and colleagues report on a randomized controlled trial evaluating the effects of a cognitive training program in persons with mild cognitive impairment (MCI). Their work is well designed and elevates the field of research in cognitive training. Their work is also timely The study that Belleville and colleagues reported found that cognitive training improves memory and increases the use of active memory strategies in daily life through 6 months of follow- up, but there was no evidence of transfer to instrumental activities of daily living (ADLs) or memory. These findings are wholly consistent with the literature and other evidence as summarized in the NAS report. The study of Belleville and colleagues has some methodological strengths that advance the field of training in cognitive aging research. One of the most important is the use of an active control group. Belleville and colleagues account for their failure to show transfer to ADLs in their sample, and their design. Future researchers should consider in their research design whether the transfer effects on distal outcomes reflect parallel disease modification effects or whether the transfer effect is expected to operate indirectly through improved cognitive performance. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
