TY  - JOUR
T1  - Decreasing fall risk: Intensive cognitive training and blood pressure control
AN  - 1933976397; 2017-22205-005
AB  - Comments on an article 
Slow Processing Speed Predicts Falls in Older Adults With a Falls History: 1-Year Prospective Cohort Study by J. C. Davis et al. (2017). Cognitive impairment is not the only condition that predicts falls in elderly adults. Declines in physical mobility and depression also increase the probability of falling. Simultaneous investigation of multiple risk factors is necessary to inform appropriate interventions to reduce falls. The article by Davis et al. address this point by investigating the predictive value of baseline measures of cognition, physical mobility, and psychological well-being in determining subsequent falls in a large community-dwelling sample of older adults with mild cognitive problems and a history of falls in the year before. They found that a composite processing speed-executive functioning (PS-EF) score was the strongest predictor of different types of falls, followed by measures of walking speed, balance, depression, and emotional well-being. These results provide further evidence of the interconnection between PS-EF, depression, and mobility and generate an important discussion on treatments that target shared etiological abnormalities. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
