TY  - JOUR
T1  - Response to Dr. Amit Lampit et al
AN  - 1760851917; 2015-57917-027
AB  - Comments on an article by Dr. Amit Lampit, Michael Valenzuela, and Nicola J. Gates (see record 2015-57917-025). The commentators agree that there is sufficient evidence to support modest benefits of group-based cognitive training, within limited populations. At the same time, the observed effects of CCT are not protective against the pathophysiology of neurodegenerative diseases. Even when cognitive training affects some of the measures used in dementia assessment, there is no evidence that it prevents or forestalls dementia. Although the commentators accept the safety and measurable effects on domain-specific cognition with low-intensity, group based cognitive training, we discourage medical providers from recommending brain games to patients as a means of preventing or forestalling dementia. We expect that, over time, the fields of neuropsychology and neuroscience will discover optimal ways to maintain brain function despite aging, but delaying onset or severity of neurodegenerative diseases may long remain elusive. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
