TY  - JOUR
T1  - Do practices of learning activities improve the cognitive functioning of healthy elderly adults? From the viewpoint of a transfer effect
AN  - 1604739196; 2014-34677-003
AB  - The present study examined influences of reading aloud and performing simple calculation on the cognitive functioning of healthy elderly adults, based on the findings that these tasks activated the prefrontal lobe. The elderly adults' memory and inhibitory functions were assesed by Short-Term memory, CST, Stroop, and SRC tasks, before and after intervention for 18 months. The study found that the learning group had significant improvement from the pre- to the post-test for the short-term memory, STM, CST, and Stroop tasks. On the other hand, there was significant decline over the 18 months in the control group which was given only the assessment tasks. These results are discussed in terms of the effectiveness of cognitive training. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2024 APA, all rights reserved)
 (Source: journal abstract)
