TY  - JOUR
T1  - The ACTIVE study: Study overview and major findings
AN  - 1492675065; 2014-00367-001
AB  - This article provides an overview of the papers featured in the present issue of 
Journal of Aging and Health. The papers collectively focus on the Advanced Cognitive Training for Independent and Vital Elderly (ACTIVE) and reports both baseline and longitudinal data from the ACTIVE study. Cognitive training has been shown to improve both cognitive and everyday abilities in older adults; however, little is known about the amount of training needed, or the characteristics of those who benefit. These analyses examined the longitudinal impact of dosage on the improvement and maintenance of cognitive and everyday function. Three articles address this issue for each of the cognitive training interventions. One of the papers aimed to better understand the effects of the ACTIVE training interventions. Another paper uses baseline data for the no-contact control group to identify predictors of instrumental activities of daily living (IADL) performance over 5 years. The ACTIVE study is the first large-scale, randomized trial to test the long-term outcomes of cognitive training effects on prevention of decline in daily function. Results support the effectiveness of cognitive intervention in maintaining cognitive health over the long-term and indicate modest but detectable far transfer to IADL, health-related quality of life (QOL), and driving outcomes. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
