TY  - JOUR
T1  - Repeated daily exposure to direct current stimulation does not result in sustained or notable side effects
AN  - 1536021321; 2014-01303-010
AB  - This study examined the safety profile associated with repeated exposure to transcranial direct current stimulation (tDCS). Forty healthy, college-aged subjects participated in this study. All participants engaged in a working memory training task each day for 10 consecutive workdays. To explore whether the experience of side effects changed over time, the four sensations identified as being significantly different by group were averaged for each session to create a side effect severity index for each session. Twenty participants received active stimulation and twenty participants received sham stimulation during each training session. An examination of the variance in these symptoms over session revealed that there were no consistent group differences in severity ratings as a function of session. This finding suggests that the experience of symptoms does not change monotonically with repeated exposure to tDCS. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
