TY  - JOUR
T1  - The reciprocal relationship of neurocognitive and neuropsychiatric function in late life
AN  - 1315881764; 2012-34471-001
AB  - This editorial presents an overview of the articles which were featured in this issue of 
The American Journal of Geriatric Psychiatry. This special issue focus on research investigations that underscore the complex relationship between cognition and psychiatric symptoms in older adults with mental illness. Certainly there is substantial rationale for a reciprocal relationship between late-life neurocognitive and neuropsychiatric function. As both the National Institute of Mental Health and Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders Fifth Edition move toward a more dimensional approach to psychiatric disorders, characterizing the full range of cognitive function is increasingly important for (1) refining late-life psychiatric phenotypes; (2) increasing our ability to predict cognitive decline among those with late-life psychiatric disorders; (3) using cognitive function to predict the onset and course of psychiatric symptoms and disorders; (4) predicting pharmacologic and psychotherapeutic treatment response; (5) developing and identifying treatment targets such as cognitive training and augmentation; and (6) offering a window to neurocircuitry and the development of transdiagnostic measures for geriatric psychiatry. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
