TY  - JOUR
T1  - Practice effects and outcome of cognitive training: Preliminary evidence from a memory training course
AN  - 742962264; 2010-06288-012
AB  - Practice effects (PE) are improvements in cognitive test performance due to repeated exposure to the tests, and these artificial improvements have traditionally been viewed as error. A logical extension of this research is to see whether PE are related to outcomes of interventions, which is the focus of this study. Twenty three community dwelling older adults completed a 5-week memory training course, based on The Memory Bible. Pretesting cognitive performances were largely in the "average "range (37th–60th percentiles), as were subjective memory complaints. PE, usually viewed as error, were related to change in cognitive test scores after a 5-week interval. To our knowledge, this is the first study to prospectively study PE in relation to treatment outcome, although we have observed that PE provide diagnostic and prognostic information. Although there are several limitations for this study, additional investigation of PE in treatment studies seems warranted. If these cognitive plasticity proxies demonstrate predictive value, then PE could enrich samples in clinical trials, leading to more effective training programs and better allocation of limited resources. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2017 APA, all rights reserved)
