TY  - JOUR
T1  - Four paradigms to study visual-spatial attention of myopic subjects
AN  - 619697052; 2001-18120-001
AB  - Describes 4 protocols for evaluating the attentional skills of myopic and control Ss in target stimulus detection tasks; simple reaction times (RT) are recorded. 96 right-handed Ss (aged 18–22 yrs), half myopic, participated. Two protocols are designed to study both automatic and voluntary orienting of attention. Modified implicit orienting paradigms are used in which cues elicit a shift of attention but gaze is maintained on a central fixation point. A third paradigm is designed to study the time-course of focusing; 2 circles (focusing cues) of different diameters are randomly presented on a point of the visual space where attention has been previously shifted. Seven stimulus onset asynchrony are used. The fourth paradigm was designed to evaluate visual search; 3 circular arrays of 4, 8, and 12 stimuli are randomly presented around a fixation point and Ss have to detect the target stimulus inside one of the circles (the other circles are distracters). Since some attentional deficits are associated with myopia, these protocols could be useful both in the detection of deficits in Ss on the verge of becoming myopics and in the development of cognitive training programs to reduce attentional deficits. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
