TY  - JOUR
T1  - Zusammenhang von Alter, kognitiven Leistungen und Bewegungskoordination
TT  - Relationship of age, cognitive performance and motor coordination
AN  - 619690198; 2001-18408-003
AB  - Analyzed the relationship of motor coordination and cognition in different age groups. 119 Ss (aged 35–90 yrs) in Germany were divided into 4 age groups (35–47 yrs, 48–60 yrs, 61–73 yrs, and 74–90 yrs). Ss were administered 3 motor coordination tests in 4 different variations. Cognitive performance was measured using a digit symbol substitution test, a memory span test and block design test as well as a color–word test. The results show a significant age effect in all of the coordination tests. Ss of the highest age group showed a clear decrease in coordinative performance. A similar age effect was found in cognitive performance: older Ss achieved fewer points in general cognition and needed more time for the color–word test. Age-adjusted correlations confirmed positive associations between coordinative performance with a high level of demand and cognitive performance. The need to include some elements of cognitive training in suitable coordination training intended to promote coordination in old age is discussed. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
