TY  - JOUR
T1  - Zur Bedeutung der sozialen Interaktion bei der Evaluation kognitiver Förderprogramme
TT  - The meaning of social interaction in evaluations of cognitive training programs
AN  - 619504649; 2000-12188-005
AB  - Tested hypotheses concerning the effects of social interaction and of coaching in 2 studies in Germany. In study 1, 49 children (aged 5 yrs 10 mo–8 yrs 3 mo) were divided in 3 intervention groups: (1) "Denktrainings-Gruppe" (DT, thought training group); (2) "DenkMit-Gruppe" (DM, think-along group); and (3) "Gedächtnis trainings-gruppe" (GT, memory training group). General intelligence and visual perception were tested immediately prior and after intervention, using the Basic Intelligence Test; Weiss & Osterland, 1980) and the (POD Visual Perception Test; Sauter, 1979). 31 children (aged 6 yrs 0 mo–7 yrs 2 mo) participated in study 2, in which, in 12 individual sessions, 15 Ss were administered a modified version of the DT (DT—M) and 16 Ss were given the GT. The results support to some extent the expected effects of social interaction and coaching with regard to general intelligence and visual perception. However, concept formation tasks show differential patterns of results under the various programs. The findings indicate that the effects of social interaction should not be eliminated as they may lead to improved performance. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
