TY  - JOUR
T1  - Computer-aided cognitive training of the aged: A pilot study
AN  - 619685364; 2001-17233-002
AB  - Compared the effects of a computer intervention on the selected cognitive abilities of 14 male nursing home residents who used a computer program vs those of 15 male residents who did not. Ss were 55–96 yrs of age; were diagnosed with varied medical conditions, including cerebral vascular accident, congestive heart failure, diabetes, dementia, and cancer; and resided at the Department of Veterans Affairs Medical Centers in the Pacific Northwest. The intervention comprised 62 levels of memory matching tests that progressed from simple to difficult. The measurement battery consisted of tests measuring 5 general dimensions: fluid intelligence, short and long term memory, attention, and general cognitive ability. Controlling for initial level of cognitive function, the study found that multivariate analysis of variance indicated significant improvement for the computer users compared with nonusers on the 5 outcome levels. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
